<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A VALID BUT EMPTY url set, and deliberately so.

  Every screen of this application requires an authenticated session, and /robots.txt
  disallows the whole origin for every agent, so there is no URL here that a crawler
  should be told to fetch. The document exists because the path used to fall through to
  the SPA history fallback and answer with the application's HTML document under HTTP 200,
  which told a sitemap consumer that markup was a sitemap.

  An entry would also have to be wrong to be written at all: the sitemap protocol requires
  <loc> to be a fully-qualified URL, and this bundle is built once and served behind
  whatever host a deployment puts in front of it, so no absolute URL is knowable here. The
  same reason is why /robots.txt names no Sitemap directive, which the protocol likewise
  requires to be absolute.
-->
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9" />
